  "subtitle": "The first computer-graphics planetarium, piloted from a 3-by-4-foot console of back-lit buttons and a six-degree-of-freedom joystick — with a button labelled 'Boldly Go'.",
  "why_scout_cares": "The Digistar was the first computer-graphics planetarium projection and content system, built by Evans & Sutherland and first installed in 1983. What makes it a museum piece rather than a display curiosity is its operator interface: the original Digistar and Digistar II had a physical control panel roughly 3 by 4 feet, crowded with a keyboard, a six-degree-of-freedom joystick, and a large array of back-lit buttons. One of those buttons, which moved the viewpoint forward through space, was labelled 'Boldly Go' — a Star Trek reference, appropriate because a Digistar laboratory prototype generated the star fields for Star Trek II: The Wrath of Khan. This is a cockpit-like, performance instrument for flying audiences through the sky, not a desktop GUI.",
  "search_terms": ["Evans & Sutherland Digistar", "Digistar planetarium 1983", "Digistar control panel", "Digistar back-lit buttons joystick", "Boldly Go button", "Star Trek II Wrath of Khan Digistar", "Stephen McAllister Brent Watson Digistar", "calligraphic CRT planetarium projector"],
