    "by": "Evans & Sutherland Computer Corporation, Salt Lake City, Utah, USA",
    "tags": ["HCI", "Graphics", "Planetarium", "Embodied", "Input", "Performance"],
    "overview": "Digistar is the first computer-graphics-based planetarium projection and content system, designed by Evans & Sutherland and released in 1983. It was the brainchild of lifelong amateur astronomers and graphics engineers Stephen McAllister and Brent Watson, who in 1977 were consulting with NASA's Johnson Space Center on astronaut training simulators. Its purpose was to overcome the key limitation of traditional opto-mechanical star balls: that they could only show the sky from the surface of the Earth. Using computer graphics, Digistar could show accurate star fields from any point in space and time. The projector itself is a single calligraphic (vector) CRT whose electron beam is driven directly to the coordinates of each star and line, painting bright, non-pixelated points onto a phosphor plate; a 160-degree lens then throws the image across the entire dome. Driven by a DEC VAX-11/780 with custom graphics hardware, the Digistar was operated from a large physical console. Its success is difficult to overstate for the sector: the first customer installation was the Science Museum of Virginia in 1983, for $595,000, and by 2019 the Digistar line had more than 550 planetarium installations worldwide.",
    "deepDive": [
      {"heading": "A cockpit, not a menu", "text": "The original Digistar and Digistar II were run from a physical control panel roughly 3 by 4 feet in size, containing a keyboard, a six-degree-of-freedom joystick, and a large array of back-lit buttons. The operator performed the show with the body — steering the viewpoint through space with the joystick, punching illuminated buttons to summon models and celestial events. It is closer to a theatre organ or an aircraft cockpit than to a graphical user interface."},
      {"heading": "'Boldly Go'", "text": "Among the back-lit buttons on the Digistar panel was one, used to move the viewpoint forward in space, labelled 'Boldly Go' — after the Star Trek catchphrase 'where no man has gone before'. The joke was earned: a Digistar laboratory prototype generated the star fields and tactical displays for Star Trek II: The Wrath of Khan (1982), and the film's opening 3.5-minute starfield fly-through became the first entirely computer-generated feature film sequence. The Digistar team members were credited in the film."},
      {"heading": "Drawing with an electron beam", "text": "The Digistar projector is a calligraphic display. Instead of scanning a raster, its single CRT drives the electron beam straight to the coordinates of each star or wireframe vertex in a display list. Because a star's phosphor spot glows longer the more often it is hit, short display lists produce very bright stars — far brighter than a raster display could, and with darker blacks, since empty sky is never struck. The result dances between the visible and the actual: a bright phosphorescent green sky that many viewers cannot distinguish from white."}
